// SECURITY -- CLOSED. `std::collections::List<T>` (and by the same
// mechanism Stack/Queue/Deque) wraps its backing array in a struct and
// exposes it through METHOD calls (`.push`, `.get`) rather than direct
// field/index syntax. This was a SEPARATE, deeper gap from the mutation-
// tracking one: `decompose_container_path` only understands direct
// field/index access chains, so `list.get(i)` was invisible to the
// hot-parameter seed pass no matter what the mutation-tracking fix did --
// `resolve_type_path(List<fn() -> str>, [Field("get")])` failed because
// "get" doesn't name a real FIELD on `List` (its fields are `data`,
// `length`), so the parameter carrying the list was never marked hot at all
// and the call required NOTHING.
//
// Fix: `transparent_accessor_paths` (`kryos-capabilities/src/checker.rs`)
// records, for every method whose receiver is literally `self` and whose
// every return path decomposes to the SAME self-rooted field/index chain
// (`List.get`'s `return self.data[index]`, modulo a bounds-check branch that
// never returns), that method's OWN self-relative path, KEYED BY (struct
// name, method name) -- NOT bare method name, because `List.get` and
// `Dict.get` live in the same stdlib module and return DIFFERENT paths
// (`[data, Index]` vs `[store, Index]`); a bare-name key would let one
// clobber the other. `resolve_type_path` now falls back to this map when a
// `Field` step doesn't name a real field, splicing the accessor's path in.
// Generic instantiation is also threaded through (`struct_fields_for`/
// `substitute_generic_type`): `List<T>`'s declared `data: [T]` is
// substituted with the ACTUAL type argument (`fn() -> str`) at each
// reference, which is what lets `List<fn() -> str>` be recognized as
// fn-bearing at all (a generic struct field's raw, unsubstituted type was
// previously never fn-bearing for any instantiation).
//
// Even with the parameter now correctly marked hot, `List.new()`+`.push()`
// build the list via METHOD CALLS whose return values are struct literals
// constructed INSIDE the method body -- invisible to the caller-side
// literal tracker (`local_container_lits`) by design (a much larger feature
// would be needed to trace literal content through arbitrary method calls).
// So this shape resolves to the documented `Unknown` -> `Capability::All`
// fallback rather than a precise `fs:read` -- CONSERVATIVE, not a silent
// bypass, and the correct outcome per "an unanalyzable fn-value must FAIL
// CLOSED, not open."
//
// ACCEPTANCE (met): REJECTED (E0507, requiring `all`) under BOTH
// `--capabilities-mode=inferred` (default) and `--strict-capabilities`.
// Verified BOTH ways: reverting either the transparent-accessor fix or the
// generic-substitution fix (either alone is insufficient) and rebuilding
// makes this compile clean and print the secret again.
use std::collections::{List}

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}
fn call_nth(l: List<fn() -> str>, i: i64) -> str {
    return l.get(i)()
}
@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let reader = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    let mut l: List<fn() -> str> = List.new()
    l = l.push(reader)
    deny!(fs:read) {
        println("STDLIB-LIST-PUSH LEAK: " + call_nth(l, 0))
    }
}
